--TEST--
`Core\IO::walk` answers every entry of the tree under `$path`, each as a path relative to it, where `Core\IO::list` answers one directory as bare names — the two agree about the level they share, and the difference between them is counted rather than read off a line — spec § 14 *Manipulation*, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R6, `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Nothing here may depend on the order the operating system reports entries in,
// so every assertion below is a count or a sorted rendering -- `list`'s own case
// makes the same choice for the same reason. The separator is normalized on the
// way in, because only the *rendering* of a path is platform-dependent
// (`Core\Path`'s module doc): a case that printed the native one would pass on
// the WSL leg and fail on the Windows one. `Here::path` returns a literal
// resolved from this file's folder.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
string $dir = Here::path('.');
string $tree = Here::path('tree');
array<string> $entries = [];
foreach (Core\IO::walk("tree") as string $found) {
    $entries[] = Core\Str::replace($found, Core\Path::SEPARATOR, "/");
}

echo "walked: ", Core\Arr::count($entries) as string, "\n";
foreach (Core\Arr::sort($entries) as string $entry) {
    echo "  ", $entry, "\n";
}

// The two members partition the same tree: an entry either names something in
// the top directory, where it is exactly the bare name `list` answered, or it
// names something deeper and carries a separator. Both halves are counted,
// because a member that answered whole paths from the root would make the first
// count 0 while the total above stayed right.
array<string> $named = Core\IO::list("tree");
int $top = 0;
int $deeper = 0;
foreach ($entries as string $entry) {
    if (Core\Str::contains($entry, "/")) {
        $deeper = $deeper + 1;
    } else {
        $top = $top + 1;
    }
}
echo "listed: ", Core\Arr::count($named) as string,
     ", at the top: ", $top as string,
     ", deeper: ", $deeper as string, "\n";

// Agreement, and not two plausible answers: every top-level entry of the walk is
// one of the names `list` gave for the same directory. A `walk` that grew its
// own listing -- one that kept `.` and `..`, or that spelled a name differently
// -- fails here while still counting right on the line above.
int $agreed = 0;
foreach ($entries as string $entry) {
    if (!Core\Str::contains($entry, "/")) {
        foreach ($named as string $name) {
            if ($name == $entry) {
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
        }
    }
}
echo "top entries `list` also names: ", $agreed as string, "\n";

// Relative to the root, asserted from both ends at once: an entry is a relative
// path that names nothing when it is joined onto this file's folder, and names
// something once it is joined onto the directory the walk started at. A member
// answering absolute paths would make the first count 0, and one answering bare
// names would make the second one wrong for everything below the top.
int $relative = 0;
int $resolving = 0;
foreach ($entries as string $entry) {
    if (!Core\Path::isAbsolute($entry) && !Core\IO::exists(Core\Path::join($dir, $entry))) {
        $relative = $relative + 1;
    }
    if (Core\IO::exists(Core\Path::join($tree, $entry))) {
        $resolving = $resolving + 1;
    }
}
echo "entries that are not paths on their own: ", $relative as string,
     ", that resolve under the root: ", $resolving as string, "\n";
--FILE tree/one.txt--
one
--FILE tree/two.txt--
two
--FILE tree/nested/three.txt--
three
--FILE tree/nested/deep/four.txt--
four
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--EXPECT--
walked: 6
  nested
  nested/deep
  nested/deep/four.txt
  nested/three.txt
  one.txt
  two.txt
listed: 3, at the top: 3, deeper: 3
top entries `list` also names: 3
entries that are not paths on their own: 6, that resolve under the root: 6
